    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero",
    "problem": "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-3}=\\frac{z-6}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y+1}{-3}=\\frac{z-6}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{z-6}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=6:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3+7t \\\\ -1-3t \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-6}{0}$ part and instead write $z=6$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y+1}{-3},\\,z=6\n$$",
